				}
				secret: true
				nbt: {
					ForgeCaps: {
						"mineminenomi:entity_stats": {
							faction: "mineminenomi:bandit"
						}
					}
				}
			}]
			rewards: [
				{
					id: "29F405C3B1E2D10D"
					type: "item"
					item: {
						id: "minecraft:written_book"
						Count: 1b
						tag: {
							pages: [
								"{\"text\":\"I was hiding under the bridge when I first saw him.\\nThe river smelled like rust and old fish, and the stones were cold even through my pants. I’d scraped my knee earlier, but I didn’t cry. Crying made noise. Noise got you found.\"}"
								"{\"text\":\"Boots stopped above me.\\n“Kid,” a man said. “You alive down there, or you just pretending?”\\nI held my breath. My fingers dug into the dirt. If I stayed quiet long enough, maybe he’d leave.\\nHe didn’t.\\n\"}"
								"{\"text\":\"A face leaned over the edge of the bridge, upside down. Dark skin, sharp eyes, a crooked grin like he’d already won something today. A black bandana was tied around his head, loose, like he didn’t care if it fell off.\\n“There you are,” he said. “Thought I smelled trouble.”\"}"
								"{\"text\":\"I crawled out before he could grab me. I stood up and tried to look big. I wasn’t big. I was thin, hungry, and shaking, but I didn’t back away.\\nHe noticed. They always did.\\n“What’s your name?” he asked.\\nI didn’t answer.\"}"
								"{\"text\":\"He squatted so we were eye to eye. He was huge up close. Scars crossed his arms like roads on a map. His coat was patched and torn, but clean in a strange way, like it mattered to him.\\n“I won’t bite,” he said. “Not unless you ask.”\\nI frowned. “That’s stupid.”\\n\"}"
								"{\"text\":\"He laughed. Loud. Real. It echoed under the bridge and made birds take off.\\n“Yeah,” he said. “You’re gonna be trouble someday.”\\nI didn’t know who he was then. I just knew people ran when they saw him. \"}"
								"{\"text\":\" I heard a woman whisper his name behind closed doors like it was a curse.\\nBoss Horya D. Tashif.\\nThe worst bandit alive, they said. The one the government couldn’t hang. The man who stole gold from palaces and bread from lords and somehow made it end up in empty hands.\"}"
								"{\"text\":\"“Why are you hiding?” he asked.\\n“Because soldiers take kids,” I said. “And food.”\\nHis smile faded. Just a little.\\n“Yeah,” he said quietly. “They do.”\\nWe heard shouting then. Boots. Metal clinking. Soldiers were crossing the bridge.\\n\"}"
								"{\"text\":\"Tashif stood up slow, like nothing in the world could rush him.\\n“How fast can you run?” he asked.\\n“Fast,” I lied.\\nHe grabbed my wrist and pulled me close. “Listen to me. When I say go, you run. Don’t look back.” \"}"
								"{\"text\":\"“What about you?”\\nHe grinned again, wider this time. “I’ll make some noise.”\\nThe soldiers appeared at the far end of the bridge. Five of them. Rifles up. Faces hard.\\n“Tashif!” one of them yelled. “Drop the kid and get on your knees!”\\n\"}"
								"{\"text\":\"Tashif sighed. “They always ruin a good talk.”\\nHe pushed me behind him.\\n“Go,” he said.\\nI didn’t.\\nThe first shot cracked the air. Stone exploded near my feet. My heart jumped into my throat. Tashif moved.\\n\"}"
								"{\"text\":\"He was everywhere at once. He grabbed a soldier’s rifle and smashed it into another man’s face. He laughed as he fought, like this was a game he’d played a thousand times and never got tired of.\\nI was supposed to stay hidden.\"}"
								"{\"text\":\"That was the first thing he told me.\\n“Don’t move,” Boss Horya D. Tashif said. “No matter what you hear.”\\nI nodded hard, like that made me brave.\\nI was crouched behind a broken cart near the bridge, hugging my knees.\"}"
								"{\"text\":\"The air smelled like wet wood and iron. My heart wouldn’t slow down. I could hear soldiers shouting somewhere close, boots hitting stone.\\nTashif stood in the open like he wanted to be seen. Black bandana tied around his head, coat loose, hands calm.\"}"
								"{\"text\":\"Too calm.\\n“Kid,” he said without looking at me, “remember what I said.”\\n“I will,” I whispered.\\nI didn’t.\\nA soldier stepped out from the trees behind him. I saw the metal of a blade catch the light. It was raised, ready to strike Tashif from behind.\"}"
								"{\"text\":\"My chest tightened.\\nHe didn’t see it.\\nI stood up.\\n“Tashif!” I yelled.\\nEverything broke at once.\\nThe soldier lunged—but not at Tashif. At me. I froze. My feet wouldn’t move.\"}"
								"{\"text\":\" The knife was suddenly very close, too close, and I understood how small I really was.\\nThen Tashif was there.\\nHe moved so fast it felt like the world jumped. He grabbed me and yanked me back against his chest. \"}"
								"{\"text\":\" The knife missed my throat by nothing.\\nIt didn’t miss him.\\nSteel tore across his face as he turned, one slash cutting deep into his cheek. He roared, not in pain, but in rage, and drove his elbow into the soldier’s skull.\"}"
								"{\"text\":\"Another soldier rushed in. I was still tangled in Tashif’s coat, still in the way.\\n“No—!” I tried to say.\\nTashif shoved me behind him again.\\nThe second blade came down.\\nThis time, it crossed the first cut.\\n\"}"
								"{\"text\":\"An X, carved into his cheek because I hadn’t listened.\\nBecause I’d moved.\\nBecause I’d been stupid.\\nTashif didn’t fall. He didn’t even step back. He grabbed the soldier by the collar and slammed him into the ground so hard the earth shook.\"}"
								"{\"text\":\"The rest of them ran.\\nWhen it was over, my ears were ringing. My hands were shaking so bad I couldn’t close them.\\nBlood ran down Tashif’s face, dripping off his jaw. He wiped it away and stared at his fingers.\\n“…Huh,” he muttered. “That’s a bad one.”\\n\"}"
								"{\"text\":\"“I’m sorry,” I said. My voice cracked. “I—I didn’t—”\\nHe turned to me.\\nFor a second, I thought he was going to yell.\\nHe didn’t.\\nHe knelt in front of me instead, big hands resting on my shoulders.\"}"
								"{\"text\":\"“Hey,” he said. “Look at me.”\\nI did. My eyes burned.\\n“That wasn’t your fault,” he said.\\n“Yes it was,” I said. “You told me not to move.”\\nHe was quiet for a moment. The river kept flowing. \"}"
								"{\"text\":\"Birds screamed somewhere far away.\\nThen he smiled. Small. Tired.\\n“Yeah,” he said. “And I told myself I was done getting scars. Guess we both lied.”\\nI shook my head. “You got hurt because of me.”\"}"
								"{\"text\":\"He reached up and touched the fresh X on his cheek. His fingers came away red.\\n“This?” he said. “This means I was where I needed to be.”\\nI didn’t believe him.\\nHe untied the black bandana from his head and pressed it to his face.\"}"
								"{\"text\":\" When he pulled it away, it was soaked through.\\nHe stared at it for a long time.\\nThen he held it out to me.\\n“Take it.”\\nI stared. “I can’t.” “You will.” \"}"
								"{\"text\":\"“My hands were shaking when I took it. The cloth was warm. Heavy. It smelled like blood and smoke and him.\\n“Why give it to me?” I asked.\\nTashif stood up and looked down at me, the X sharp and clear on his cheek now.\\n\"}"
								"{\"text\":\"“Because one day,” he said, “you’re gonna blame yourself for a lot of things. Some you should. Some you shouldn’t.”\\nHe tapped the scar.\\n“This one’s mine,” he said. “But you’ll remember it anyway. That’s how it works.”\"}"
								"{\"text\":\"He turned to leave.\\n“I’m sorry!” I shouted after him. “I won’t forget!”\\nHe didn’t turn around.\\n“Good,” he said. “Don’t.”\\nI tied the bandana around my arm after he was gone.\"}"
								"{\"text\":\"It was too big.\\n It smelled like iron.\\nAnd every time I looked at it, I saw that X—\\n and remembered the moment I learned what it meant to be protected.\\n\"}"
							]
